    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "b) Figure 11: Isostatic dyadic grids for N = 0 and N = 1."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 96.907, 348.451, 317.94, 357.418 ],
    "font" : "CMR9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "and infinitesimally rigid configuration by Theorem 6.3."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 96.907, 304.616, 498.37, 346.459 ],
    "font" : "CMR9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Notice for the purpose of the recursion that the segments ab and cd have points with dyadic coordinates at level N and crossings, by single lines, exactly at heights (2k + 1)/2N+1, with 0 ≤ k ≤ 2N −1. Notice also that segments ae, eb, bf and fc have points exactly those with dyadic coordinates at level N + 1, and are only crossed by lines at those points."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 96.907, 238.862, 498.372, 302.624 ],
    "font" : "CMR9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "For the recursive step start by the points of intersection of ab and cd with the horizontal lines of the configuration. Add the lines between each of the new points and p3 and p4. Each such new line bisects one of the segments along ae, eb, bf and fc. Add points at each of these intersections. Finally, add horizontal lines through the new points along ae and eb and vertical lines through the new points along bf and fc. These subdivide the grid, with the points along ab and cd again alternating between points and pairs of crossed lines."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 96.907, 205.986, 498.371, 236.87 ],
    "font" : "CMR9",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The dyadic grids at levels N = 0 and N = 1 are illustrated in Figures 11b and 11a. Each level is created from the previous by intersections of two lines and drawing lines through two points, and so each level is independent and infinitesimally rigid by Theorem 6.3."
  }, {
    "type" : "table",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Table",
    "id" : 16,
    "level" : "3",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 491.622, 208.227, 497.948, 214.448 ],
    "number of rows" : 1,
    "number of columns" : 1,
    "rows" : [ {
      "type" : "table row",
      "row number" : 1,
      "cells" : [ {
        "type" : "table cell",
        "pdfua_tag" : "TD",
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 492.021, 208.427, 497.55, 214.248 ],
        "row number" : 1,
        "column number" : 1,
        "row span" : 1,
        "column span" : 1,
